\section{Sign check}{


The engine's WOE is event-oriented, so every glm coefficient must be
positive. A variable with a non-positive coefficient (or above
\code{max_abs_coef} in absolute value) is explaining what another already
explained, with the sign reversed; it is removed and the model refitted,
one at a time, the most negative first, and each removal is recorded in
\code{sign_check}. The last remaining variable is never removed: it is kept
and flagged \code{NON_POSITIVE_COEF_KEPT_LAST}.
The final shortlist of the scorecard (\code{features}) is what \code{\link[=scr_sql]{scr_sql()}}
covers.
}

\section{Points per bin}{


With \code{score = a + b * logit} and \code{logit = alpha + sum(beta_j * woe_ij)}:
\deqn{\mathrm{points}_{ij} = b\,\beta_j\,\mathrm{woe}_{ij},\qquad
      \mathrm{base} = a + b\,\alpha.}
\code{points_style = "distributed"} spreads \code{base / k} over each
characteristic (Siddiqi, 2006, chapter 6), leaving \code{base_points = 0}. The
exact points stay in \code{points_raw}; \code{points} is the rounded version when
\code{points_round = TRUE}. The exact score (\code{score}) and the whole-points
score (\code{score_points}) are both returned by \code{\link[=scr_apply]{scr_apply()}} and both
emitted by \code{\link[=scr_sql]{scr_sql()}}. A row that falls in no fitted bin (a category
never seen on train, a missing value without a missing bin) gets a WOE of
0 from the binning engine, hence the points of a WOE of 0: \code{0}, or
\code{base / k} under \code{"distributed"}.
}

